00:11:06,680 --> 00:11:09,940
And in the spring of 1865, Union cavalry

279
00:11:09,940 --> 00:11:11,300
swept across Alabama.

280
00:11:11,800 --> 00:11:13,680
They burned ironworks and arsenals.

281
00:11:14,020 --> 00:11:15,800
They burned most of the University of Alabama.

282
00:11:16,700 --> 00:11:21,980
Spring 1865, the war ends, and 435,000

283
00:11:21,980 --> 00:11:23,760
people in Alabama are free.

284
00:11:24,520 --> 00:11:26,560
Freedom changed everything, fast.

285
00:11:27,280 --> 00:11:29,940
Within five years, Alabama sent its first Black

286
00:11:29,940 --> 00:11:32,740
congressman to Washington, Benjamin Turner of Selma.

287
00:11:33,140 --> 00:11:34,480
He had been born enslaved.

288
00:11:35,540 --> 00:11:36,920
It did not last.

289
00:11:37,560 --> 00:11:40,020
By 1901, white leaders wrote a new state

290
00:11:40,020 --> 00:11:40,680
constitution.

291
00:11:41,120 --> 00:11:42,480
They did not hide why.

292
00:11:42,960 --> 00:11:44,700
The man in charge of the convention told

293
00:11:44,700 --> 00:11:47,540
the delegates the goal was, quote, to establish

294
00:11:47,540 --> 00:11:49,240
white supremacy in this state.

295
00:11:50,120 --> 00:11:53,040
Poll taxes and literacy tests followed, and the

296
00:11:53,040 --> 00:11:53,980
voting rolls emptied.

297
00:11:53,980 --> 00:11:57,500
Most Black Alabamians and many poor white Alabamians

298
00:11:57,500 --> 00:11:58,380
lost the vote.

299
00:11:59,100 --> 00:12:01,860
And that constitution was amended so many times,

300
00:12:02,200 --> 00:12:04,720
nearly a thousand times, that it became the

301
00:12:04,720 --> 00:12:06,460
longest constitution in the world.

302
00:12:06,900 --> 00:12:08,980
Seriously, it's longer than most novels.

303
00:12:09,960 --> 00:12:12,700
But underground, something else was waiting.

304
00:12:13,420 --> 00:12:15,360
Near the middle of the state, there's a

305
00:12:15,360 --> 00:12:16,600
ridge called Red Mountain.

306
00:12:17,020 --> 00:12:18,540
It turned out to hold iron ore.

307
00:12:18,540 --> 00:12:20,140
Right next to it, coal.

308
00:12:20,500 --> 00:12:22,020
And next to that, limestone.

309
00:12:22,760 --> 00:12:25,220
Iron ore, coal, and limestone are the three

310
00:12:25,220 --> 00:12:26,540
things you need to make iron.

311
00:12:27,020 --> 00:12:29,340
Finding all three in one place is incredibly

312
00:12:29,340 --> 00:12:29,660
rare.

313
00:12:30,280 --> 00:12:33,360
So in 1871, investors built a brand new

314
00:12:33,360 --> 00:12:35,140
city right on top of them, and named

315
00:12:35,140 --> 00:12:38,040
it after the great English industrial city, Birmingham.

316
00:12:38,740 --> 00:12:40,880
It grew so fast that people called it

317
00:12:40,880 --> 00:12:41,840
the Magic City.

318
00:12:42,580 --> 00:12:45,560
To show off, Birmingham cast a giant iron

319
00:12:45,560 --> 00:12:48,000
statue of Vulcan, the Roman god of the

320
00:12:48,000 --> 00:12:50,440
forge, for the 1904 World's Fair.

321
00:12:50,960 --> 00:12:52,280
56 feet tall.

322
00:12:52,700 --> 00:12:54,640
Still the largest cast iron statue in the

323
00:12:54,640 --> 00:12:54,880
world.

324
00:12:55,560 --> 00:12:57,940
When the fair ended, Birmingham couldn't pay the

325
00:12:57,940 --> 00:12:59,920
shipping bill, so the god of the forge

326
00:12:59,920 --> 00:13:02,280
was dumped in pieces beside the railroad tracks.

327
00:13:03,040 --> 00:13:04,760
Today he stands on top of Red Mountain,

328
00:13:05,100 --> 00:13:07,080
with his bare backside pointed straight at the

329
00:13:07,080 --> 00:13:07,780
neighboring suburb.

330
00:13:08,380 --> 00:13:10,480
But the Magic City had a dark engine

331
00:13:10,480 --> 00:13:10,740
room.

332
00:13:10,740 --> 00:13:13,680
Thousands of Black men were arrested for tiny

333
00:13:13,680 --> 00:13:16,600
offenses like vagrancy, and then leased out to

334
00:13:16,600 --> 00:13:18,460
coal mines and work camps as prisoners.

335
00:13:19,240 --> 00:13:20,680
They worked in terrible conditions.

336
00:13:21,340 --> 00:13:21,860
Many died.

337
00:13:22,560 --> 00:13:24,380
Alabama was the last state to end this

338
00:13:24,380 --> 00:13:26,300
system in 1928.

339
00:13:27,000 --> 00:13:30,140
Meanwhile, in the town of Tuskegee, a former

340
00:13:30,140 --> 00:13:31,380
slave named Booker T.

341
00:13:31,540 --> 00:13:33,680
Washington had opened a school for Black students.

342
00:13:34,500 --> 00:13:37,680
In 1896, he hired a quiet scientist named

343
00:13:37,680 --> 00:13:39,240
George Washington Carver.

344
00:13:40,160 --> 00:13:42,280
Carver looked at the tired cotton fields and

345
00:13:42,280 --> 00:13:43,800
said, plant peanuts.

346
00:13:44,140 --> 00:13:45,620
They put goodness back into the soil.

347
00:13:46,200 --> 00:13:48,980
Then he found hundreds of uses for them.

348
00:13:49,520 --> 00:13:52,200
Dyes, milk, plastics, cosmetics.

349
00:13:53,000 --> 00:13:55,240
And no, he did not invent peanut butter.

350
00:13:55,620 --> 00:13:55,860
Sorry.

351
00:13:57,100 --> 00:13:58,840
Farmers did not want to listen.

352
00:13:59,260 --> 00:14:00,180
Cotton was money.

353
00:14:00,860 --> 00:14:04,040
Then in the 1910s, a tiny beetle crawled

354
00:14:04,040 --> 00:14:04,800
in from Mexico.

355
00:14:05,320 --> 00:14:06,340
The boll weevil.

356
00:14:07,140 --> 00:14:08,080
It ate the cotton.

357
00:14:08,700 --> 00:14:11,040
Field after field, whole counties were ruined.

358
00:14:12,040 --> 00:14:13,940
So in the town of Enterprise, the farmers

359
00:14:13,940 --> 00:14:15,320
finally switched to peanuts.

360
00:14:15,860 --> 00:14:17,420
And they started making more money than they

361
00:14:17,420 --> 00:14:18,360
ever had from cotton.

362
00:14:19,320 --> 00:14:21,760
So in 1919, the grateful people of Enterprise

363
00:14:21,760 --> 00:14:23,580
did something no town on earth had ever

364
00:14:23,580 --> 00:14:23,820
done.

365
00:14:24,580 --> 00:14:25,480
They built a monument.

366
00:14:26,180 --> 00:14:26,800
To the bug.

367
00:14:27,480 --> 00:14:29,840
It's still there, in the main street.

368
00:14:30,400 --> 00:14:32,560
A woman holding a giant boll weevil over

369
00:14:32,560 --> 00:14:32,980
her head.

370
00:14:33,440 --> 00:14:35,300
The only monument in the world to an

371
00:14:35,300 --> 00:14:36,240
agricultural pest.

372
00:14:37,540 --> 00:14:38,120
And just one more.

373
00:14:38,540 --> 00:14:40,760
In 1910, the Wright brothers came to an

374
00:14:40,760 --> 00:14:43,880
old cotton plantation outside Montgomery and opened America's

375
00:14:43,880 --> 00:14:45,420
first civilian flying school.

376
00:14:46,060 --> 00:14:48,300
Their students made the first night flights in

377
00:14:48,300 --> 00:14:48,680
history.

378
00:14:49,780 --> 00:14:52,960
Alabama farmers were watching airplanes before most Americans

379
00:14:52,960 --> 00:14:54,220
had ever seen one.

380
00:14:54,740 --> 00:14:55,440
And the people.

381
00:14:56,480 --> 00:14:59,580
Alabama kept producing people the whole world would

382
00:14:59,580 --> 00:14:59,840
know.

383
00:15:00,760 --> 00:15:03,840
In 1880, in the town of Tuscumbia, a

384
00:15:03,840 --> 00:15:05,720
baby girl was born who would lose her

385
00:15:05,720 --> 00:15:07,660
sight and her hearing before she was two.

386
00:15:08,600 --> 00:15:11,160
Years later, at a water pump in her

387
00:15:11,160 --> 00:15:14,300
family's garden, her teacher spelled a word into

388
00:15:14,300 --> 00:15:17,380
her hand, over and over, while cold water

389
00:15:17,380 --> 00:15:18,560
ran across the other one.

390
00:15:19,160 --> 00:15:22,840
W-A-T-E-R.

391
00:15:24,200 --> 00:15:27,180
That was the moment Helen Keller understood that

392
00:15:27,180 --> 00:15:28,340
things have names.

393
00:15:29,000 --> 00:15:30,760
She went on to write books and travel

394
00:15:30,760 --> 00:15:31,180
the world.

395
00:15:32,220 --> 00:15:33,600
And the list keeps going.

396
00:15:34,160 --> 00:15:36,800
Jesse Owens, who won four gold medals in

397
00:15:36,800 --> 00:15:38,400
Berlin in front of Hitler.

398
00:15:38,920 --> 00:15:40,040
Joe Louis, the boxer.

399
00:15:40,640 --> 00:15:42,760
Hank Aaron and Willie Mays, two of the

400
00:15:42,760 --> 00:15:44,520
greatest baseball players ever.

401
00:15:45,080 --> 00:15:47,840
Nat King Cole, Hank Williams, all of them

402
00:15:47,840 --> 00:15:48,920
born in Alabama.

403
00:15:51,570 --> 00:15:53,290
And many of them grew up somewhere else.

404
00:15:53,930 --> 00:15:56,830
Because in the early 1900s, hundreds of thousands

405
00:15:56,830 --> 00:15:59,510
of Black Alabamians packed up and left for

406
00:15:59,510 --> 00:16:00,670
the factories of the North.

407
00:16:01,090 --> 00:16:04,610
For Chicago, Detroit, and Cleveland, looking for work

408
00:16:04,610 --> 00:16:06,370
and for a life without Jim Crow.

409
00:16:08,490 --> 00:16:09,050
1931.

410
00:16:09,810 --> 00:16:11,570
A freight train in northern Alabama.

411
00:16:12,590 --> 00:16:15,870
Nine Black teenagers, the youngest about 13, are

412
00:16:15,870 --> 00:16:17,790
pulled off it and accused of a crime

413
00:16:17,790 --> 00:16:18,630
they did not commit.

414
00:16:19,630 --> 00:16:22,590
In the town of Scottsboro, all white juries

415
00:16:22,590 --> 00:16:24,390
sentenced eight of them to death.

416
00:16:25,310 --> 00:16:27,550
One of their accusers later took her story

417
00:16:27,550 --> 00:16:27,930
back.

418
00:16:28,730 --> 00:16:30,610
The case went to the United States Supreme

419
00:16:30,610 --> 00:16:33,450
Court twice, and it changed the law on

420
00:16:33,450 --> 00:16:35,410
fair trials for everyone in America.

421
00:16:36,090 --> 00:16:38,510
But the young men lost years of their

422
00:16:38,510 --> 00:16:39,390
lives in prison.

423
00:16:40,450 --> 00:16:42,910
Alabama officially pardoned the last of them in

424
00:16:42,910 --> 00:16:43,590
2013.

425
00:16:45,130 --> 00:16:46,550
Remember the name Scottsboro.

426
00:16:47,190 --> 00:16:49,770
It comes back in a very different way.

427
00:16:50,250 --> 00:16:52,730
Now keep the Wright brothers in mind, because

428
00:16:52,730 --> 00:16:55,410
the sky is about to become Alabama's thing.

429
00:16:56,310 --> 00:17:00,750
In 1941, at Tuskegee, the Army began training

430
00:17:00,750 --> 00:17:03,030
America's first Black military pilots.

431
00:17:03,990 --> 00:17:06,030
Plenty of people in charge expected them to

432
00:17:06,030 --> 00:17:06,290
fail.

433
00:17:07,610 --> 00:17:10,330
Nearly a thousand pilots earned their wings there.

434
00:17:11,110 --> 00:17:13,010
In the war over Europe, they painted the

435
00:17:13,010 --> 00:17:15,510
tails of their planes red, and bomber crews

436
00:17:15,510 --> 00:17:18,050
started asking for the red tails by name.

437
00:17:19,130 --> 00:17:21,150
But Tuskegee holds a darker story too.

438
00:17:22,490 --> 00:17:26,350
Starting in 1932, government doctors studied hundreds of

439
00:17:26,350 --> 00:17:28,210
Black men in the area who had syphilis.

440
00:17:28,970 --> 00:17:30,690
The men were never told what they had.

441
00:17:31,570 --> 00:17:34,550
When penicillin became the cure, the men were

442
00:17:34,550 --> 00:17:35,369
not given it.

443
00:17:36,290 --> 00:17:38,830
The study ran for 40 years, until a

444
00:17:38,830 --> 00:17:40,670
reporter exposed it in 1972.

445
00:17:42,090 --> 00:17:45,410
In 1997, the President of the United States

446
00:17:45,410 --> 00:17:46,889
apologized to the survivors.

447
00:17:48,369 --> 00:17:48,990
1941.

448
00:17:50,170 --> 00:17:51,129
War again.

449
00:17:52,090 --> 00:17:54,830
The Army builds a giant weapons plant next

450
00:17:54,830 --> 00:17:56,630
to a quiet cotton town in the north

451
00:17:56,630 --> 00:17:57,450
called Huntsville.

452
00:17:57,990 --> 00:17:59,990
Its name is Redstone Arsenal.

453
00:18:00,830 --> 00:18:03,490
When the war ends, the arsenal goes quiet.

454
00:18:04,650 --> 00:18:07,050
Huntsville is about to lose its biggest employer.

455
00:18:08,190 --> 00:18:11,450
And then, in 1950, the Army sends over

456
00:18:11,450 --> 00:18:13,670
a hundred German engineers to Huntsville.

457
00:18:14,490 --> 00:18:16,670
Huntsville at the time had about 16,000

458
00:18:16,670 --> 00:18:19,790
people, and proudly called itself the watercress capital

459
00:18:19,790 --> 00:18:20,450
of the world.

460
00:18:21,070 --> 00:18:23,690
And these strangers had come to build rockets.

461
00:18:24,330 --> 00:18:26,310
Their leader was Wernher von Braun.

462
00:18:27,090 --> 00:18:29,150
In Germany, he had built the V-2,

463
00:18:29,450 --> 00:18:31,950
the world's first long-range ballistic missile.

464
00:18:32,390 --> 00:18:34,730
That is the part of the story Huntsville

465
00:18:34,730 --> 00:18:36,110
took years to face.

466
00:18:36,950 --> 00:18:38,770
Von Braun had been a member of the

467
00:18:38,770 --> 00:18:41,350
Nazi party and an officer in the SS.

468
00:18:42,090 --> 00:18:44,410
His V-2 rockets were built underground by

469
00:18:44,410 --> 00:18:46,270
prisoners from a concentration camp.

470
00:18:46,870 --> 00:18:48,590
Thousands of those prisoners died.

471
00:18:49,150 --> 00:18:51,190
More people died building the V-2 than

472
00:18:51,190 --> 00:18:52,110
were killed by it.

473
00:18:52,810 --> 00:18:55,130
After the war, the United States wanted his

474
00:18:55,130 --> 00:18:56,970
knowledge more than it wanted answers.

475
00:18:57,550 --> 00:18:59,730
Questions about his past came much later.

476
00:19:00,610 --> 00:19:02,430
In Huntsville, the team got to work.

477
00:19:03,150 --> 00:19:04,370
January 1958.

478
00:19:05,070 --> 00:19:07,350
A rocket built by the Huntsville team launches

479
00:19:07,350 --> 00:19:10,350
Explorer 1, America's very first satellite.

480
00:19:10,350 --> 00:19:11,350
1960.

481
00:19:12,090 --> 00:19:14,250
NASA opens the Marshall Space Flight Center at

482
00:19:14,250 --> 00:19:14,690
Redstone.

483
00:19:15,250 --> 00:19:17,650
Von Braun is its first director, and they

484
00:19:17,650 --> 00:19:19,950
start drawing the biggest rocket the world had

485
00:19:19,950 --> 00:19:23,390
ever seen, the Saturn V, taller than the

486
00:19:23,390 --> 00:19:25,670
Statue of Liberty, built to take people to

487
00:19:25,670 --> 00:19:26,130
the moon.

488
00:19:27,170 --> 00:19:30,130
The watercress town filled up with engineers, mathematicians,

489
00:19:30,210 --> 00:19:30,850
and machinists.

490
00:19:31,330 --> 00:19:33,950
Within 10 years, Huntsville's population had more than

491
00:19:33,950 --> 00:19:34,590
quadrupled.

492
00:19:34,590 --> 00:19:36,630
They called it the Rocket City.

493
00:19:37,310 --> 00:19:40,250
But a few hours south in Montgomery, on

494
00:19:40,250 --> 00:19:42,890
our street, something else was about to launch.

495
00:19:43,730 --> 00:19:44,330
1954.

496
00:19:45,210 --> 00:19:47,590
A young preacher arrives to lead a small

497
00:19:47,590 --> 00:19:50,550
brick church on Dexter Avenue, one block down

498
00:19:50,550 --> 00:19:51,470
the hill from the Capitol.

499
00:19:52,150 --> 00:19:53,770
He is 25 years old.

500
00:19:54,270 --> 00:19:57,490
His name is Martin Luther King Jr. In

501
00:19:57,490 --> 00:19:59,710
Montgomery, the city buses had a rule.

502
00:20:00,230 --> 00:20:02,570
Black passengers paid at the front, got off,

503
00:20:02,890 --> 00:20:04,630
walked around, and got back on at the

504
00:20:04,630 --> 00:20:05,110
back door.

505
00:20:05,710 --> 00:20:07,870
And if a white passenger needed a seat,

506
00:20:08,170 --> 00:20:09,830
a Black passenger had to stand.

507
00:20:10,630 --> 00:20:13,610
In March 1955, a 15-year-old girl

508
00:20:13,610 --> 00:20:15,690
named Claudette Colvin refused to give up her

509
00:20:15,690 --> 00:20:15,910
seat.

510
00:20:16,410 --> 00:20:17,010
She was arrested.

511
00:20:18,010 --> 00:20:20,830
Nine months later, on December 1st, a seamstress

512
00:20:20,830 --> 00:20:23,030
finished work at a department store and walked

513
00:20:23,030 --> 00:20:24,930
to the bus stop, right at the bottom

514
00:20:24,930 --> 00:20:27,930
of our five blocks, on Court Square, where people

515
00:20:27,930 --> 00:20:28,930
had once been sold.

516
00:20:29,710 --> 00:20:31,470
Her name was Rosa Parks.

517
00:20:32,050 --> 00:20:33,570
When the driver told her to stand up,

518
00:20:33,990 --> 00:20:35,010
she said, no.

519
00:20:35,810 --> 00:20:38,330
Four days later, Black Montgomery stopped riding the

520
00:20:38,330 --> 00:20:38,610
buses.

521
00:20:39,250 --> 00:20:41,770
People walked to work, miles, every day, in

522
00:20:41,770 --> 00:20:42,210
the rain.

523
00:20:42,610 --> 00:20:43,910
They organized carpools.

524
00:20:44,510 --> 00:20:46,650
And the boycott was planned in that little

525
00:20:46,650 --> 00:20:49,190
church on Dexter Avenue, with a 26-year-old

526
00:20:49,190 --> 00:20:50,690
Dr. King as its leader.

527
00:20:51,310 --> 00:20:54,110
They kept it up for 381 days.

528
00:20:54,110 --> 00:20:56,390
And in the end, the Supreme Court ruled

529
00:20:56,390 --> 00:20:59,250
that Montgomery's segregated buses were against the Constitution.

530
00:21:00,230 --> 00:21:02,610
The bus boycott was only the first round.

531
00:21:03,510 --> 00:21:07,170
In 1963, Alabama's new governor, George Wallace, stood

532
00:21:07,170 --> 00:21:09,390
on the Capitol steps, on the same spot

533
00:21:09,390 --> 00:21:11,730
where Jefferson Davis had been sworn in, and

534
00:21:11,730 --> 00:21:14,330
promised, quote, segregation forever.

535
00:21:15,090 --> 00:21:16,990
That summer, he stood in a doorway at

536
00:21:16,990 --> 00:21:19,130
the University of Alabama to block two Black

537
00:21:19,130 --> 00:21:20,030
students from enrolling.

538
00:21:20,530 --> 00:21:22,170
He stepped aside that same day.

539
00:21:22,830 --> 00:21:25,870
In Birmingham, thousands of children, some still in

540
00:21:25,870 --> 00:21:27,770
grade school, marched for their rights.

541
00:21:28,430 --> 00:21:30,410
The police met them with dogs and fire

542
00:21:30,410 --> 00:21:30,770
hoses.

543
00:21:31,350 --> 00:21:33,090
The pictures went around the world.

544
00:21:33,770 --> 00:21:35,830
September 15, 1963.

545
00:21:36,530 --> 00:21:38,590
A Sunday morning in Birmingham.

546
00:21:39,650 --> 00:21:42,410
A bomb, planted by Ku Klux Klan members,

547
00:21:42,530 --> 00:21:45,190
exploded at the 16th Street Baptist Church.

548
00:21:46,130 --> 00:21:47,570
Four girls were killed.

549
00:21:48,430 --> 00:21:52,610
Addie Mae Collins, Carole Robertson, Cynthia Wesley, Denise

550
00:21:52,610 --> 00:21:53,050
McNair.

551
00:21:53,950 --> 00:21:55,010
Denise was 11.

552
00:21:55,510 --> 00:21:56,830
The others were 14.

553
00:21:57,730 --> 00:21:58,690
Then came Selma.

554
00:21:59,210 --> 00:22:02,370
In Dallas County, around Selma, more than half

555
00:22:02,370 --> 00:22:04,910
the people were Black, but only about two

556
00:22:04,910 --> 00:22:07,230
out of every hundred eligible Black voters were

557
00:22:07,230 --> 00:22:07,630
registered.

558
00:22:08,390 --> 00:22:10,230
Every time people lined up at the courthouse

559
00:22:10,230 --> 00:22:13,010
to register, they were turned away, or arrested.

560
00:22:13,870 --> 00:22:17,250
So they decided to march, 54 miles, from

561
00:22:17,250 --> 00:22:19,730
Selma all the way to Montgomery, to the

562
00:22:19,730 --> 00:22:22,250
Capitol, to the top of our five blocks.

563
00:22:23,130 --> 00:22:25,550
Sunday, March 7, 1965.

564
00:22:26,410 --> 00:22:29,790
About 600 marchers walk out of Selma, two

565
00:22:29,790 --> 00:22:32,410
by two, and climb the Edmund Pettus Bridge.

566
00:22:33,070 --> 00:22:35,270
At the bottom, state troopers are waiting.

567
00:22:36,030 --> 00:22:38,310
The troopers charge with clubs and tear gas.

568
00:22:38,840 --> 00:22:41,730
A young organizer named John Lewis has his

569
00:22:41,730 --> 00:22:42,410
skull fractured.

570
00:22:43,010 --> 00:22:45,270
That night, the whole country saw it on

571
00:22:45,270 --> 00:22:45,690
television.

572
00:22:46,350 --> 00:22:47,950
It became known as Bloody Sunday.

573
00:22:48,690 --> 00:22:51,550
Two weeks later, they tried again, this time

574
00:22:51,550 --> 00:22:53,110
protected by federal troops.

575
00:22:53,970 --> 00:22:56,150
Five days, 54 miles.

576
00:22:56,690 --> 00:22:58,310
They slept in fields along the highway.

577
00:22:58,970 --> 00:23:02,510
And on March 25, around 25,000 people

578
00:23:02,510 --> 00:23:05,430
marched up Dexter Avenue, past the fountain where

579
00:23:05,430 --> 00:23:07,910
people were once sold, past the bus stop

580
00:23:07,910 --> 00:23:11,130
where Rosa Parks said no, past Dr. King's

581
00:23:11,130 --> 00:23:13,710
church, up the hill to the steps where

582
00:23:13,710 --> 00:23:15,030
the Confederacy was born.

583
00:23:15,790 --> 00:23:18,530
That August, the president signed the Voting Rights

584
00:23:18,530 --> 00:23:18,810
Act.

585
00:23:20,230 --> 00:23:21,170
Five blocks.

586
00:23:21,810 --> 00:23:24,130
Slavery at the bottom, the Confederacy at the

587
00:23:24,130 --> 00:23:24,350
top.

588
00:23:24,970 --> 00:23:27,290
And a hundred years later, the people the

589
00:23:27,290 --> 00:23:30,230
Confederacy was built to keep down walked up

590
00:23:30,230 --> 00:23:33,390
those same five blocks and took the steps.

591
00:23:34,070 --> 00:23:38,030
Now cut back to Huntsville, July 16, 1969.

592
00:23:38,970 --> 00:23:41,850
In Florida, the Saturn V, the rocket designed

593
00:23:41,850 --> 00:23:44,970
in Alabama, lifts off with three astronauts on

594
00:23:44,970 --> 00:23:45,250
top.

595
00:23:47,090 --> 00:23:50,730
Four days later, a human being steps onto

596
00:23:50,730 --> 00:23:51,090
the moon.

597
00:23:52,290 --> 00:23:54,850
Four years after Bloody Sunday.

598
00:23:55,490 --> 00:23:56,010
Same state.

599
00:23:57,030 --> 00:23:59,110
So here is the answer to the question

600
00:23:59,110 --> 00:23:59,950
from the very beginning.

601
00:24:00,450 --> 00:24:02,170
How does one state do both?

602
00:24:03,050 --> 00:24:04,890
Because the rocket and the bridge were the

603
00:24:04,890 --> 00:24:06,130
same kind of story.

604
00:24:06,890 --> 00:24:09,350
Ordinary people in Alabama decided that the line

605
00:24:09,350 --> 00:24:11,950
everyone said they could never cross was not

606
00:24:11,950 --> 00:24:12,590
the end of the road.

607
00:24:13,430 --> 00:24:14,650
One group did it with engineering.

608
00:24:15,210 --> 00:24:16,710
The other did it with their feet.

609
00:24:17,590 --> 00:24:19,250
And a third group did it with a

610
00:24:19,250 --> 00:24:19,530
song.

611
00:24:20,370 --> 00:24:22,430
Remember that town of 14,000 people from

612
00:24:22,430 --> 00:24:23,050
the very start?

613
00:24:23,570 --> 00:24:25,470
Muscle Shoals on the Tennessee River in the

614
00:24:25,470 --> 00:24:25,870
far north?

615
00:24:26,370 --> 00:24:28,910
In the 1960s, a little studio there called

616
00:24:28,910 --> 00:24:30,510
FAME started making hits.

617
00:24:31,190 --> 00:24:33,390
Wilson Pickett recorded Mustang Sally there.

618
00:24:33,890 --> 00:24:36,950
In 1967, a young singer named Aretha Franklin

619
00:24:36,950 --> 00:24:39,290
walked in, sat down at the piano, and

620
00:24:39,290 --> 00:24:40,750
cut the song that made her a star.

621
00:24:41,730 --> 00:24:43,530
The house band were local white musicians.

622
00:24:44,130 --> 00:24:45,730
They called themselves the Swampers.

623
00:24:46,450 --> 00:24:47,870
In a state that was fighting to keep

624
00:24:47,870 --> 00:24:49,910
Black and white people apart, they were in

625
00:24:49,910 --> 00:24:52,090
a room every day making Black soul music

626
00:24:52,090 --> 00:24:52,870
with Black singers.

627
00:24:53,830 --> 00:24:55,670
The story goes that when one famous New

628
00:24:55,670 --> 00:24:57,830
York songwriter tried to hire the Black musicians

629
00:24:57,830 --> 00:24:59,510
he'd heard on those records, he was told

630
00:24:59,510 --> 00:25:00,990
they were four white guys from Alabama.

631
00:25:02,030 --> 00:25:04,850
And in December 1969, five months after the

632
00:25:04,850 --> 00:25:07,470
moon landing, the Rolling Stones quietly drove into

633
00:25:07,470 --> 00:25:10,230
town and recorded Brown Sugar and Wild Horses

634
00:25:10,230 --> 00:25:10,450
there.

635
00:25:10,890 --> 00:25:12,490
Most of the locals had no idea.

636
00:25:13,470 --> 00:25:15,810
And the five blocks have one more stop.

637
00:25:17,070 --> 00:25:17,670
1979.

638
00:25:18,730 --> 00:25:21,790
George Wallace, the man who promised segregation forever,

639
00:25:22,390 --> 00:25:24,850
now in a wheelchair after an assassination attempt,

640
00:25:24,850 --> 00:25:27,530
asked to be wheeled into Dr. King's old

641
00:25:27,530 --> 00:25:28,610
church on Dexter Avenue.

642
00:25:29,710 --> 00:25:32,150
In front of the congregation, he apologized.

643
00:25:32,790 --> 00:25:35,290
He said he had learned what suffering means.

644
00:25:36,050 --> 00:25:37,330
And he asked for their forgiveness.

645
00:25:38,510 --> 00:25:39,570
Some people forgave him.

646
00:25:40,310 --> 00:25:41,030
Some never did.

647
00:25:41,870 --> 00:25:43,030
Both are fair.

648
00:25:43,870 --> 00:25:45,170
But he went back.

649
00:25:45,590 --> 00:25:48,030
And he asked, on that same street.

650
00:25:48,430 --> 00:25:50,410
And Alabama kept moving.

651
00:25:50,990 --> 00:25:54,330
In 1997, Mercedes-Benz opened its first American

652
00:25:54,330 --> 00:25:56,850
car factory in a small Alabama town called

653
00:25:56,850 --> 00:25:57,330
Vance.

654
00:25:57,870 --> 00:26:01,310
Then came Honda, Hyundai, Toyota, and Mazda.

655
00:26:01,910 --> 00:26:04,510
Today, Alabama's factories can build more than a

656
00:26:04,510 --> 00:26:05,610
million cars a year.

657
00:26:06,110 --> 00:26:07,830
The SUV in the parking lot outside?

658
00:26:08,230 --> 00:26:10,050
There's a real chance it was built here.

659
00:26:10,870 --> 00:26:11,970
Oh, and remember Scottsboro?

660
00:26:12,450 --> 00:26:15,410
In 1970, a man there started buying lost

661
00:26:15,410 --> 00:26:17,150
luggage that nobody had ever claimed.

662
00:26:17,590 --> 00:26:19,570
Today, when the airlines give up on a

663
00:26:19,570 --> 00:26:21,710
bag, it ends up at his family's store.

664
00:26:22,130 --> 00:26:23,130
Unclaimed Baggage.

665
00:26:23,850 --> 00:26:25,830
About a million people a year drive to

666
00:26:25,830 --> 00:26:28,570
a small Alabama town to shop for strangers'

667
00:26:28,790 --> 00:26:29,370
suitcases.

668
00:26:30,670 --> 00:26:33,630
Laptops, wedding dresses, somebody's skis.

669
00:26:34,150 --> 00:26:35,990
If you ever lost a bag, it might

670
00:26:35,990 --> 00:26:37,630
be on a shelf in Alabama right now.

671
00:26:38,590 --> 00:26:41,570
Airbus builds passenger jets in Mobile, the city

672
00:26:41,570 --> 00:26:43,890
that still throws America's oldest Mardi Gras.

673
00:26:44,450 --> 00:26:46,750
The little town that called itself the Watercress

674
00:26:46,750 --> 00:26:49,170
Capital is now Alabama's biggest city.

675
00:26:49,650 --> 00:26:52,250
And in 2025, the government announced that the

676
00:26:52,250 --> 00:26:55,210
military's Space Command headquarters would move to Huntsville,

677
00:26:55,410 --> 00:26:55,510
too.

678
00:26:56,830 --> 00:27:00,490
In 2019, researchers confirmed they had found the

679
00:27:00,490 --> 00:27:02,590
wreck of the Clotilda, buried in the mud

680
00:27:02,590 --> 00:27:03,470
of the Mobile River.

681
00:27:04,030 --> 00:27:06,070
The descendants of the people it carried still

682
00:27:06,070 --> 00:27:08,850
live nearby, in a neighborhood their ancestors built

683
00:27:08,850 --> 00:27:09,490
after the war.

684
00:27:10,030 --> 00:27:11,530
It's called Africatown.

685
00:27:12,590 --> 00:27:15,410
And in April 2026, a new moon rocket

686
00:27:15,410 --> 00:27:17,150
lifted off with four astronauts.

687
00:27:17,730 --> 00:27:19,370
It was designed and managed at the Marshall

688
00:27:19,370 --> 00:27:20,730
Space Flight Center in Huntsville.

689
00:27:21,230 --> 00:27:23,030
They flew around the moon and came home,

690
00:27:23,390 --> 00:27:25,370
farther from Earth than any human beings had

691
00:27:25,370 --> 00:27:25,890
ever been.

692
00:27:26,710 --> 00:27:28,290
Alabama sent people around the moon.

693
00:27:28,910 --> 00:27:29,270
Again.

694
00:27:30,350 --> 00:27:31,390
And on the ground?

695
00:27:32,130 --> 00:27:34,970
Every autumn, half the state still stops talking

696
00:27:34,970 --> 00:27:36,890
to the other half for one football game.

697
00:27:37,390 --> 00:27:38,850
Alabama against Auburn.

698
00:27:39,370 --> 00:27:40,550
They call it the Iron Bowl.

699
00:27:41,310 --> 00:27:42,170
Of course they do.

700
00:27:42,790 --> 00:27:44,310
Birmingham was built on iron.

701
00:27:45,230 --> 00:27:47,110
So let's go back to that name.

702
00:27:48,010 --> 00:27:48,470
Alabama.

703
00:27:49,090 --> 00:27:51,450
Remember the newspaper that said it meant, here

704
00:27:51,450 --> 00:27:52,030
we rest?

705
00:27:52,410 --> 00:27:54,390
Nobody has ever found those words in the

706
00:27:54,390 --> 00:27:54,690
language.

707
00:27:55,150 --> 00:27:57,450
The most common explanation is that it comes

708
00:27:57,450 --> 00:27:59,730
from Choctaw words about clearing the land.

709
00:28:00,150 --> 00:28:00,950
Clearing the thicket.

710
00:28:00,950 --> 00:28:03,110
The people who clear the thicket.

711
00:28:03,890 --> 00:28:06,110
Ten thousand years of people clearing a path

712
00:28:06,110 --> 00:28:06,910
where there wasn't one.

713
00:28:07,330 --> 00:28:08,270
With a cave fire.

714
00:28:08,590 --> 00:28:09,170
With a peanut.

715
00:28:09,570 --> 00:28:10,330
With a rocket.

716
00:28:10,810 --> 00:28:11,550
With a bus seat.

717
00:28:11,930 --> 00:28:13,350
With a march across a bridge.

718
00:28:14,030 --> 00:28:16,250
That is the entire history of Alabama.

719
00:28:17,190 --> 00:28:18,570
Never here we rest.

720
00:28:19,150 --> 00:28:20,670
Always clear the path.
